WEBVTT

NOTE Edited for clarity from Joseph's recorded narration; timed to the submitted 180-second cut.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.100
A green build can still be a broken handover.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.500
All eight new-version checks pass.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.050
But an old worker is still writing while the database changes.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.800
This late bridge looks safe after the migration.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.000
All 76 completed-rollout probes pass.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.500
Now we replay old writes between migration statements.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.850
Sixteen window probes fail.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.700
Here is the missed window.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.800
We add the new column and copy existing rows.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.650
Then an old worker writes before synchronization starts.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.000
The write is accepted.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.950
But the new reader sees a stale value.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000
IBM Bob used Cutover's live tools

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.200
to trace the lost old-worker write.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:20.600
Its first repair failed: two triggers called each other recursively. We kept that failure.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.100
Bob removed the reverse trigger and moved the one-way bridge before backfill.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.400
The new writer got explicit dual writes.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.800
That protects the old reader during rollback.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.550
We independently replayed Bob's saved Parcel and Warehouse plans against the unchanged SQLite evaluator.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:58.950
Each passed 116 of 116 probes.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:06.950
Bob used four migration statements, so its window count differs from the five-statement reference.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.600
The live app reruns the plan before showing a verdict.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.350
Bob also built the pull-request gate. Live GitHub checks

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:24.750
blocked unsafe and regressed plans, while the safe control passed.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.800
Each run retained its exact report, verdict, witness, and review bundle.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.650
This prewritten trap passes every migration window.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.350
Then a second write erases the first.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.650
Cutover catches that cross-record loss.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.800
Cutover rehearses schema changes in SQLite.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.100
Concurrent transactions and production databases remain future work.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.900
That is why Cutover is useful for database developers.
